;;; The twin of `wild-in-head.ein` — M1e2 **W20**, banked by S1e2.2.20
;;; (2026-11-05): the same program with the head wildcard written out as the
;;; disjunction over the relations it has that a one-argument fact can
;;; belong to, `holds` and `hit`. A fresh variable is no twin for a head (M1
;;; binds a relation head by the activator, Q29). `w` fires on `(holds a)`,
;;; root holds `(hit w)`, and R is `{(hit w)}` and `{(hit a), (hit w)}`
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `c73a0796`, release and debug). It was the witness's
;;; right answer until the user ruled Q-M1e2.14 (c) on 2026-11-05: a head `_`
;;; is refused at load, so the witness's right answer is that refusal, and
;;; this is the answer of the program written out, which loads.

(relation holds Node :why "{?1} holds") (relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (or (holds a) (hit a)) :assert (hit w) :why "a holds or a is hit, so w is hit")
